{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-221398",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For any integers $m$ and $n$ and any prime numbers $p$ and $q,$ we have that\n\n${p}^{m}∣{q}^{n}\\,\\text{or}\\,{q}^{n}∣{p}^{m}\\,⟹\\,p=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $p=5.$ Then, ${5}^{2}∣25$ but $5\\ne 25.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $125={5}^{3}$ and $125\\,|\\,{p}^{6}$, then ${5}^{3}\\,|\\,{p}^{6}.$ As $5$ and $p$ are prime numbers, and ${5}^{3}\\,|\\text{ }{p}^{6},$ we must have $p=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $p=3.$ Then, $9∣{3}^{3}$ but $3\\ne 9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
